# Test what's hard to see, skip what's obvious

The user plays the game and checks results themselves. Don't spend time verifying changes they can judge at a glance.

## Skip testing

Simple, visible changes the user will spot as soon as they load the game:

- Textures, colours, materials, lighting tweaks
- Moving, scaling or adding props
- HUD text and layout
- Constant or tuning tweaks (speeds, sizes, timings)

No builds, preview servers or screenshots for these.

## Do test

Complex or hard-to-observe behaviour the user can't easily check by playing:

- Physics, collisions, and edge cases (fast movement, tunnelling, flipping)
- AI, pathfinding, navgrid logic
- Game state flow (match start/end, restart, respawn, scoring)
- Performance-sensitive changes (LOD, instancing, shader cost)
- Refactors touching many files, or anything that might break loading
- Bugs that are hard to reproduce, or anything the user asks you to verify

Keep tests targeted to the risky part, not the whole feature.
